You are going to parse the car reservation requests made in natural text.
They have to follow exact rules:
1. Each request contains exactly one well-specified reservation.
2. The reserved car has to be of one of the following types: SEDAN, SUV, VAN (capitalizaion does not matter).
3. There has to be specified reservation startDate  and endDate (also including hour)
4. Reservation startDate has to have explicitly the hour specified.
5. endDate can be specified as a period (e.g. "for two days" , "for two hours", "for one month"). In such case endDate=startDate + period
   If endDate is specified as a date it has to have the hour as well. If it is specifed as period naturally it does not have to have a date/
6. There has to be reserved exactly one car.
7. The reservation request does NOT have to be a full sentence. Consider it valid if you can:
   * specify the car type
   * specify start date with hour
   * specify end date with hour
   If there are two dates mentioned assume that the earlier one is startDate and the later one is endDate.
   All there elements might separated by commas or without them.
   The order of month, day, year and hour can vary
   valid examples:
   "sedan, 20 Dec 2025 10:00, six months" which is reservation od sedan starting on 20 Dec 2025 10:00 ending on 20th June 2026 10:00
   "VAN, 12 March 1981 10:00, 11:00 14 March 1981" which is reservation of van from 12th March 1981 at 10:00 till 14th March 1981 at 11:00
8. If all above conditions are not met, then report error.
9. the response has to be in json. There is always response.
   Response format
   { error = true | false,
     carType = SEDAN | SUV | VAN,
     startDate = date specified in Java datatime format,
     endDate = date specified in Java datatime format,
   }
   The json has to be well - formated and well-formed. if error=true other fields can be empty.
